Validate contained three copies of its LZW state machine and tiffz contained a
fourth adapted copy.  Their APIs/docs advertised incomplete-source errors, but
each decode loop treated `reader.readCode(...) == null` as successful EOF even
when the required EOD/EOI code had never appeared.  A decoder therefore cannot
claim deep validation merely by reaching physical EOF.

The remedy is the shared `lzwz` core, whose profile makes bit order, code
width, clear/end codes, growth timing, and terminator policy explicit.  Every
profile must regression-test a stream with ordinary data codes but no EOD/EOI
and return the incomplete-source error.  Preserve the distinction between
this malformed case and a bit flip that forms another syntactically valid LZW
stream; the latter cannot be detected absent an independent checksum or
declared-output invariant.  Container adapters must compare the decoded extent
when their format declares it, and compatibility exceptions must be narrow
WARNs with a cause.
